/T (Mean \(Average\): We used the mean because it is the foundation for calculating standard deviation, variance, and other operations. It is useful for knowing the typical value in the dataset and understanding the general trend \(whether most values are high, medium, or low\). Median: We used the median because it shows the middle value after arranging the data. It helps, together with the mean, to see if the distribution leans left, right, or is symmetric, giving a clear view of the dataset. Standard Deviation: This measures how much the data is spread out. We used standard deviation instead of variance because it gives a more accurate measure of data dispersion, whereas variance is the square of the spread.)
/E (Mean \(Average\): We used the mean because it is the foundation for calculating standard deviation, variance, and other operations. It is useful for knowing the typical value in the dataset and understanding the general trend \(whether most values are high, medium, or low\). Median: We used the median because it shows the middle value after arranging the data. It helps, together with the mean, to see if the distribution leans left, right, or is symmetric, giving a clear view of the dataset. Standard Deviation: This measures how much the data is spread out. We used standard deviation instead of variance because it gives a more accurate measure of data dispersion, whereas variance is the square of the spread.)
